---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../../styles/service.css";

const SITE = "https://www.tmdesignengineering.com";
const url = `${SITE}/work/water-rocket`;
const jsonld = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "@id": `${url}#article`,
    headline: "Water Rocket, a first-principles flight model, validated to ±5%",
    description: "Case study: modelling a water-powered rocket's flight path from first principles, thermodynamics, compressible nozzle flow and variable-mass kinematics, then coupling the parameters and optimising the design entirely in software. Predicts experiment within about 5% across fill, pressure and firing angle.",
    url,
    author: { "@id": `${SITE}/#thomas-mckee` },
    publisher: { "@id": `${SITE}/#business` },
    about: "Water rocket flight-dynamics model",
    isPartOf: { "@id": `${SITE}/#website` },
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Work", item: `${SITE}/#work` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Water Rocket", item: url },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Water Rocket Case Study, First-Principles Flight Model | Thomas McKee"
  description="Case study: a water-powered rocket modelled from first principles, thermodynamics, compressible nozzle flow and variable-mass flight dynamics, then optimised entirely in software. Predicts experiment within about 5% across fill, pressure and firing angle."
  type="website"
  jsonld={jsonld}>
<main>
  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="svc-hero">
    <div class="wrap svc-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Case Study · Independent Project</span>
        <h1>A water rocket, flown thousands of times, <em>in software.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">A demonstration of the method I trust most: model the physics from first principles, validate it hard against real measurement, then let the model do the searching. I built a thermodynamic, compressible-flow and flight-dynamics model of a water rocket, validated it to within about 5% of experiment, and optimised the whole design in simulation, from a single built launcher.</p>
        <div class="svc-hero-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#contact">Start your project <span class="arw">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/#work">More work</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="spec" aria-label="Project summary">
        <div class="spec-head"><span>Project</span><span>CASE 04</span></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Role</div><div class="spec-v">Designer &amp; performance lead</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Domain</div><div class="spec-v">Thermofluids · flight dynamics</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Tools</div><div class="spec-v">MATLAB · Runge–Kutta (4,5)</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Built</div><div class="spec-v">1 launcher</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Validation</div><div class="spec-v">±5% vs experiment</div></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HEADLINE -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="headline">
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">±5%</div><div class="hl">of experiment across fill, pressure and firing angle</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">&gt;300 m</div><div class="hl">range with the heaviest payload the launcher could carry</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">3</div><div class="hl">original contributions to the published model</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">0</div><div class="hl">optimisation prototypes, thousands of parameter sets, all in simulation</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PROBLEM -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Problem</span><span class="phase-cs">Water rocket flight model</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Mathematically modelling the flight path from first principles, to test thousands of parameters without thousands of launches</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:72ch">
        <p>Building and flying hundreds of design variants would have been slow, far less repeatable, and continually confounded by wind. So I simulated the trajectories in <strong>MATLAB</strong> from a <strong>mathematical model</strong> instead, but a model is only worth having if its assumptions are declared openly. I set them all out first, together with the theory each one rests on, before any equation appeared: Bernoulli through the nozzle, a polytropic gas expansion fitted from published experimental data rather than assumed isothermal, a constant <strong>drag coefficient</strong> taken from wind-tunnel results, and, the largest single divergence from reality, no wind, which is the first thing physical testing had to quantify.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/rocket-model.png" alt="First-principles thermofluid model: variable-mass motion, polytropic pressure ODE, compressible nozzle flow and exit Mach number equations" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 71, Flight phases and the free body diagram, reproduced from sketch.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/rocket-launch.png" alt="A water rocket launching from a field, plume of water trailing beneath it" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 77, The single built launcher, used to validate the model rather than to iterate the design.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- METHOD -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase phase-solution">Solution</span><span class="phase-cs">First-principles model, three derivations</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">General motion, the air thrust phase, and coupling the parameters</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:72ch">
        <p><b>Water thrust.</b> Newton's second law on a variable-mass vehicle, resolved into path coordinates, with the drag group collapsed to a single constant. Thrust comes from the momentum flux of the jet, with the exhaust velocity from Bernoulli. Here the model parts company with the literature: I treated the air expansion as <b>polytropic rather than isothermal</b>, which, via the chain rule and the polytropic relation, leaves a single ODE in pressure alone, cheap to integrate alongside the equations of motion.</p>
        <p><b>Air thrust.</b> After blow-out the vessel is a fixed-volume reservoir of compressed air, and I <b>split the air phase into choked and unchoked regimes</b>, the throat sits at Mach one until ambient rises above the critical pressure, then the exit Mach number is set by the pressure ratio. Treating the whole air phase as unchoked, as most published models do, discards the pressure-thrust term exactly where thrust is at its highest. I also <b>carried the mass of the air still inside the vessel</b> in the vehicle mass, which the standard treatments neglect.</p>
        <p><b>Coupled optimisation and structure.</b> The vessel is a thin-walled cylinder, so a <strong>structural analysis</strong> of hoop stress governs the allowable pressure, and specifying wall thickness as a fraction of radius collapses that to <span class="mono">P_all = 2βσ_y / SF</span>, independent of radius. That means radius is free to be optimised for volume and drag while the <strong>safety factor</strong> still caps the fill pressure. In a <strong>coupled optimisation</strong> I wrote the parameters as linked functions so an illegal combination can't be evaluated by accident, fitted the optimum firing angle as a function of pressure (rather than sweeping it inside every loop), and found the wall-thickness design point where the vessel first survives the cartridge pressure.</p>
        <p>Validated against both my own experimental results and the published data, the model predicted more closely than the reference model, holding accuracy to within roughly 5% across fill, pressure and firing angle alike.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/rocket-validation.png" alt="Graph of predicted range versus experiment, my model tracking the experimental points within 5% and beating the literature model" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 72, Experiment against the literature models and against mine.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/rocket-range-surface.png" alt="3D response surface of maximum horizontal range against initial pressure and firing angle" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 73, Range against pressure and firing angle.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- RESULTS -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Results</span><span class="phase-cs">Water rocket flight model</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Over 300 metres with the heaviest payload</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:72ch">
        <p>The optimised launcher was made safe and delivered its heaviest deliverable payload over 300&nbsp;metres, while the structural analysis set the allowable pressure with a safety factor, so the design was already compliant with the relevant standards before it was ever pressurised. The number matters less than the method, though: an empirically validated model let me test thousands of parameter combinations, where the alternative would have been to build hundreds of prototypes and test them one at a time. It's the same method I used on the Volt 2 heating elements and firmware five years later, and against a production deadline, establish the physics, validate against measurement, then let the model do the searching.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW I HELP -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">How I could help you</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">First-principles thinking, on your product</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Building a validated model to answer a question before committing money is exactly what a <a href="/services/feasibility-study" style="color:var(--ember)">Feasibility Study</a> and <a href="/services/design-verification" style="color:var(--ember)">Design Verification</a> are for. If your problem is thermal, fluid or dynamic and the honest answer is "we don't know yet", that's precisely the kind of thing I like to pin down.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="start">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta">
        <div class="inner">
          <span class="eyebrow">Let's talk</span>
          <h2 style="margin-top:14px">Got a question worth modelling?</h2>
          <p>Book a free intro call and tell me what you need to know before you commit. If it can be modelled and validated, it can usually be answered far more cheaply than by building.</p>
          <div class="row">
            <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/#contact">Book a free intro call <span class="arw">→</span></a>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg" href="/#work">See more work</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

<style>
  .headline{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:1px;background:var(--line);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden}
  @media(max-width:760px){.headline{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}}
  .hstat{background:var(--surface);padding:22px 20px}
  .hn{font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:2rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-0.03em;color:var(--ink)}
  .hl{color:var(--ink-2);font-size:.9rem;margin-top:6px;line-height:1.4}
  .callout{border:1px solid var(--line-2);border-left:3px solid var(--ember);background:var(--surface);border-radius:4px;padding:14px 18px;margin-top:24px;font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:13px;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.6;max-width:72ch}
  .callout b{color:var(--ink)}
  .figs{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:16px;margin-top:28px}
  @media(max-width:640px){.figs{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
  .fig{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--surface);margin:0}
  .fig img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}
  .fig figcaption{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-3);padding:9px 12px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);line-height:1.5}
  .mono{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.92em}
</style>
</Base>
